@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · STANDING" m="TKC: TL · TR · DR3 · CR; VOL. I · {v1:designations} · {v1:sorted} · {v1:elig} · {v1:assess}"
# The clerk's version and the true one
rec:
On the ledger | Covenant Daughter of Aurem, independent submission, outer districts, Sector Seven, first (TL). Selected second by the Governor-Prelate after ranking first. The woman he picked first withdrew the night before departure, for what was called a family health emergency, and it wasn't one (TR).
At the Assessment | Her toe rests exactly behind the gold tile that separates candidates from everyone else. Asked about it: "They let me through the door. Figured the floor came with it." A nail she'd hammered flat that morning rises through the cardboard into her stocking while the board's read, and she stays where she is. Her eyes go to the bottom of the board first, to Domestic Service, Binding and Remedial, before she looks up (DR3).
What she studies | The Anointed Court wives, by name, relationship and known preference, for three years (CR). The Arrangement, which Celeste told her about (CR). She can't study Lyris, because Lyris has spent thirty years making sure there's nothing to study (CR).
Why she entered | For her family, and for the girls in the outer districts who'll watch the boards and need to see her name on them (TR). Partly to be somewhere useful before a Crown education officer reaches Solomon (MEN).
OPEN QUESTION | Which track did her Designation put her on? Eligibility asks for "Unbound or elevated designation" (TR).
prompt: What she actually does all day in Aurelia, hours, rooms and hands | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: TR · TL · DR3 · DOM; VOL. I · {v1:teaches} · {v1:lived} · {v1:lived6} · {v1:scoring}"
# What she believes
rec:
What she believes | Celeste taught her that how you treat people who can't help you is the truest thing about you, and Eden holds it as faith more than strategy (TR). Her faith is real, and it "required more maintenance" than Maribel Vale's (DR3).
What she also believes | For women like her, the only road to the kind of power that changes things runs through a man willing to let her stand behind him while she works. Celeste taught her that, the Salt Ceremony teaches it, and the Empire confirms it (TR).
Who taught her | Celeste, at the kitchen table (TL). Miriam gave her a word from the Psalms and left (TL).
What she can recite | Enough to post the highest Doctrinal Assessment score in the Trials' history, on the most complex set of questions the Vowed have built in eleven cycles (TR).
What she'd say in public | Her Testimony, delivered without a handler: a statement of faith in women's intelligence worded so precisely in theological language that flagging it would mean flagging the theology (TR).
What she'd be arrested for | The house name. The real name of the city (TL).
empire: What the Temple teaches about a woman like her
Self-nomination is framed as "exceptional faith," and the process is built to exhaust anyone without backing (TR). When a candidate's intelligence becomes the story instead of her compliance, the Broadcast calls it the Eve problem and runs a narrative that "implies" and never accuses (TR). Bell prayed that God would close every door not intended for her (DR3).
/end
prompt: How she prays: the words, the posture, and where | fill

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="GEN 2–3; PS 46; HAB 3; ESTH 4.14; GEN 38; PROV 31.29; LUKE 1.52; JUDG 4.4; ROM 16.1–7; GEN 3.6"
# The text
rec:
Eden | The garden of Genesis 2–3. CONTESTED: The Hebrew *ʿeden* sounds like "delight," and some scholars connect the name to an older word for a plain or steppe.
Selah | Seventy-one times in the Psalms and three in Habakkuk. The timeline says it means "pause" (TL), the usual gloss. CONTESTED: Nobody knows what it meant. It's probably a musical or liturgical marker.
Eve · Genesis 3 | POSSIBLE: The Broadcast's own name for her problem (TR). **Where it breaks:** Eve is never asked what she wanted. Eden is asked constantly, on camera, and graded on the answer (Vol. I, {v1:eve}).
Esther | POSSIBLE: A young woman taken in a state search, prepared for months, who goes in unsummoned. **Where it breaks:** Esther is taken and told to hide her people. Eden filed the paperwork herself and paid ten dollars to be searched.
Tamar · Genesis 38 | POSSIBLE: A woman with no standing who collects evidence instead of accusing. **Where it breaks:** Tamar had a law on her side that wasn't being enforced. Eden's law is against her (Vol. I, {v1:echoes}).
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her in the Temple |
| Esther 4.14, "for such a time as this," knowing the Empire quotes it too | Judges 4.4, Deborah judging Israel with no emergency offered as an excuse |
| Luke 1.52, "He hath put down the mighty from their seats" | Romans 16.1–7, Phoebe the deacon and Junia among the apostles |
| _ | Genesis 3.6, "her husband with her," standing there the whole time |
small: Proverbs 31.29 was read over her before the scores, in King James wording (DR3).
box: Volume One
Eve, 370. Tamar, 373. Esther and the woman of worth, 386. Deborah, 376. Mary's song, 390. The two beginnings, 242. Psalms, 121. The Lord's Prayer, 415. The Covenant Trials, 542.
/end

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · BODY AND FEAR" m="TKC: DR3 · TR · TL"
# Body and fear
rec:
What the record shows | The nail through the cardboard into her stocking, and she doesn't move. Ten of her eleven dollars, counted by a clerk who inspects both sides of every bill. She walks home in the same shoes and says nothing until Celeste finds the receipt (DR3).
How she arrives | She looks at the city before she looks at the cameras, carries her own bag, and walks Sovereign Boulevard slower than the choreography, like a woman who means to walk it (TR). The Broadcast uses forty-seven seconds of it (TR).
What fear does | Fear sends her eyes to the bottom of the board first (DR3). INTERPRETATION: Her ambition is defensive before it's aspirational.
What she's afraid is true | INFERENCE: The road through a man is the Empire's design and not a loophole in it. If that's true, using it makes her the Empire's best argument.
prompt: What her body does when she lies, and what it does when she's believed | fill
prompt: What she'd do if she knew it was true and had one night to act | fill

