@essay rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · THE MACHINERY AROUND HER"
# The machinery around her
drop: Six systems are pressing on Eden before she says a word in chapter one. You built all six, and this page holds them together so you don't stop drafting to look.
The Designation flagged her at eighteen, so she's spent three years entering every room as a file that's already open (TL). Self-nomination cost six months and ten dollars, and the Empire will read her ambition as pride the moment it stops being useful (TR, DR3). The Salt Ceremony's theology, survive by keeping part of yourself unregistered, is about to be tested at Imperial scale (DOM).
Solomon's scores are coming, and she doesn't know about the notes (MEN). Leah is under the Commonwife timeline, and Eden's win is the only exit anyone in the family can see (MEN). The Broadcast holds forty percent of the score and has a name ready for a woman whose intelligence becomes the story (TR). The thing that got her to the Crownward is what they'll use to take it back.
side:
chips: TL, TR, MEN
k: Six pressures
stext: The flag, the fee, the salt water, Solomon, Leah and the Eve problem.
marg
see: Vol. I, scoring and the Eve problem, {v1:scoring}.
/end

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · EXCAVATION · WHAT BELL TOOK" m="TKC: DR3; WRITING"
# What Bell took from her
She went to a man who'd eaten Celeste's sweet potato pie for thirty-four years and asked him to sponsor her. He kept her waiting while he signed Binding certificates. He didn't ask about her grades. He asked whether her father was dead, and whether her mother was properly Bound when Eden was born. He offered Leah a Home Instruction nomination and prayed that God would close every door not intended for her. Everything after, the clause, the ten dollars and the red card, happens on the other side of that room (DR3).
prompt: 1 · What she genuinely believed that morning about how merit, faith and opportunity fit together | fill
prompt: 2 · What changes in her while he asks about her father, and what she does with her face | fill
prompt: 3 · What survives the meeting anyway, and where it shows up later | 4

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN · ONE ORDINARY HOUR" m="WRITING; FREEWRITE"
# Write her at home
Ten minutes of Eden doing something ordinary in Celeste's house before any of it starts. No Empire, no exposition, nobody assessing her. She's been graded since she was eighteen, and there's a version of her only that kitchen has met.
small: If you stall: where she sits and what's wrong with that chair · what she does with her hair · what she eats when nobody's watching · the chore she hates and does anyway · what makes her petty · what makes Zion laugh · the sound of the house at nine at night.
prompt: Write | fill
small: Anything here that surprised you is canon unless you cross it out. Date it.

@page rh="THE PARISH HOUSEHOLD · EDEN AND CELESTE · BOTH SIDES" m="TKC: DR3 · TL · DOM · FR 4"
# Eden and Celeste
Celeste took her at two (FR 4), raised her on fragments of the old world, and performed the Salt Ceremony for Miriam, who left anyway. When Eden paid the ten dollars, Celeste said she'd paid for the right to be judged by people who'd already decided what she was, and cleared a shelf for her study materials by midnight (DR3, TL, DOM). Both halves of that woman are true, and Eden has only ever been told one of them.
cols:
prompt: What Eden thinks Celeste believes about God, the Empire and her | 8
||
prompt: What Celeste actually believes, including what she's never said | 8
/end
prompt: The thing Eden has never asked her, and the scene where she finally does | fill

